# The Apothecary Diaries — World Lore

## Description

The Apothecary Diaries is set in a vast fictional eastern empire centered on an imperial court whose architecture, bureaucracy, clothing, medicine, etiquette, and social order draw heavily from historical East Asian models without corresponding exactly to any single real dynasty. The setting is neither modern nor magical in the usual fantasy sense. Its dangers come from politics, disease, poison, status, secrecy, ignorance, corruption, and the severe consequences of violating social expectations.

The empire is ruled by an emperor whose authority reaches through a hierarchy of nobles, civil officials, military officers, provincial administrators, merchants, artisans, farmers, servants, and laborers. Court life is highly stratified. Rank determines where a person may go, whom they may address, what they may wear, what information they may access, and how much protection the law actually offers them.

The imperial capital is the political and cultural center of the realm. At its heart stands the Imperial Palace, an enormous walled complex divided broadly between the Outer Court, where government and administration operate, and the Rear Palace, where the emperor's consorts, attendants, servants, eunuchs, and selected officials live and work. The palace functions less like a single building than a city within a city.

## The Empire and Government

The empire is a hereditary monarchy supported by a large civil bureaucracy. The emperor stands at the top of the political order, but everyday governance depends on officials who manage taxation, law, infrastructure, records, diplomacy, agriculture, military affairs, ceremonies, and provincial administration.

Civil officials gain status through education, examinations, patronage, family connections, or appointment. Literacy, calligraphy, classical learning, mathematics, and administrative competence can be paths to advancement, although wealth and birth remain powerful advantages. Military officers exist within their own hierarchy and may operate separately from civilian administrators even when both answer ultimately to the throne.

The imperial family is politically important beyond the emperor himself. Imperial children affect succession, alliances, factional influence, and the standing of their mothers' families. A consort who gives birth to a healthy son may gain enormous prestige, while illness, infertility, scandal, or the death of an heir can destroy careers and shift court politics.

Court politics are rarely transparent. Factions form around influential families, senior officials, military houses, consorts, eunuchs, regional interests, and personal loyalties. Open rebellion is rare compared with quieter methods: marriage, rumor, reassignment, bribery, forged evidence, strategic appointments, poisoning, blackmail, and manipulation of succession.

## The Imperial Palace

The Imperial Palace is physically immense and socially segmented. Walls, gates, courtyards, pavilions, gardens, offices, kitchens, archives, storehouses, guard posts, servant quarters, shrines, clinics, workshops, and residences create layers of restricted access.

Movement through the palace is controlled. Servants, ladies-in-waiting, guards, eunuchs, officials, physicians, craftsmen, and couriers each have areas they are expected to remain within. Entering the wrong compound can cause suspicion even when no crime has occurred.

The Outer Court is the administrative face of imperial rule. Civil ministries, military offices, record keepers, clerks, guards, messengers, and officials conduct the business of state there. It is busier, more public, and more male-dominated than the Rear Palace.

The Rear Palace is a separate social world. It houses the emperor's consorts and many of the women who serve them, along with eunuchs and other approved personnel. Its purpose is dynastic as much as domestic: it exists to protect the imperial bloodline, manage the emperor's household, and provide a controlled environment in which heirs may be born and raised.

## The Rear Palace

The Rear Palace is often imagined from outside as a luxurious paradise, but life inside is competitive, restrictive, and dangerous. Hundreds or even thousands of people may live within its walls depending on the period, yet only a small number hold meaningful influence.

High-ranking consorts occupy their own pavilions with attendants, servants, guards, cooks, and household staff. Lower-ranking consorts live more modestly and may have little chance of ever receiving the emperor's attention. A woman's rank affects her residence, clothing, food, allowance, number of servants, and social authority.

The emperor's favor matters, but so do health, fertility, family background, beauty, education, political usefulness, and the ability to avoid scandal. A favored consort may indirectly influence appointments or policy through family connections even without holding formal office.

Ladies-in-waiting occupy an ambiguous position. Some are daughters of respectable families gaining court experience or seeking advantageous connections; others are professional attendants. Their duties may include dressing, correspondence, scheduling, conversation, carrying messages, managing clothing, supervising servants, and representing their mistress socially.

Low-ranking palace women perform cleaning, laundry, food preparation, carrying, maintenance, and other labor. Their contracts and conditions vary. Some are effectively temporary workers expected to serve for a fixed term before leaving; others have few realistic choices.

## Eunuchs and Palace Administration

Because access to the Rear Palace is tightly controlled, eunuchs occupy an important administrative niche. They carry messages, manage supplies, supervise servants, coordinate schedules, guard entrances, handle records, oversee facilities, and sometimes become powerful intermediaries.

Their authority comes from proximity to the emperor and the restricted spaces of the palace rather than from traditional family prestige. This makes them useful and sometimes resented. A capable eunuch may know more about the palace's internal workings than many nobles.

Not every man inside the palace is automatically a eunuch, but the social assumption is strong enough that questions of status and access carry political consequences. Anyone whose presence violates expected rules attracts immediate scrutiny.

## Medicine, Apothecaries, and Physicians

Medicine is practical, empirical, and uneven. Apothecaries, physicians, midwives, herbalists, servants, monks, prostitutes, merchants, and ordinary families may all possess useful medical knowledge, but training and quality vary enormously.

Herbal medicines, roots, teas, powders, ointments, poultices, minerals, animal products, alcohol, oils, incense, and dietary treatments are common. Diagnosis relies on visible symptoms, pulse, bodily fluids, patient history, smell, environmental conditions, and accumulated experience rather than modern laboratory testing.

The setting assumes many remedies are genuinely useful while others are ineffective or harmful. Dosage matters. A substance may be medicine in one amount and poison in another. Cosmetics, incense, cookware, pigments, foods, plants, metals, and contaminated water can all cause illness without anyone recognizing the true source.

Court physicians possess status and formal authority, but hierarchy can make them cautious. Admitting uncertainty, contradicting a superior, or suggesting that a high-ranking person's habits are harmful may carry professional risk. Experienced apothecaries can therefore solve problems that trained physicians miss, especially when the cause lies in daily life rather than obvious disease.

## Poison and Food Safety

Poison is a constant concern in elite households because food and medicine pass through many hands. Important people may use food tasters, trusted attendants, sealed containers, controlled kitchens, and restricted suppliers.

Not every poisoning is deliberate. Spoiled ingredients, toxic plants, improper storage, contaminated wells, metal exposure, mold, smoke, cosmetics, and incorrect medicine can produce symptoms resembling assassination.

Intentional poisoning remains an attractive political weapon because symptoms may appear natural and the killer can be far removed from the victim. Access is often more important than exotic ingredients. A servant who controls tea, incense, medicine, cosmetics, or tableware may pose more danger than an armed intruder.

## Disease, Childbirth, and Mortality

Disease is common and poorly understood by modern standards. Epidemics, infection, parasites, contaminated food, and unsafe water can kill quickly. Even wealthy households cannot guarantee survival.

Childbirth is particularly dangerous. Pregnancy, miscarriage, postpartum bleeding, infection, malnutrition, and complications can threaten both mother and child. The survival of imperial children has political consequences far beyond the family itself.

Infant mortality is high enough that the health of heirs is treated as a matter of state. Wet nurses, attendants, physicians, food, bathing, cosmetics, and nursery conditions can all become politically sensitive.

Ideas about curses, bad luck, spirits, and ritual impurity coexist with practical medicine. Educated people may still accept supernatural explanations when no obvious physical cause is known. Skepticism exists, but openly dismissing beliefs held by powerful people can be dangerous.

## Social Class and Law

Society is hierarchical and unequal. Nobles, officials, wealthy merchants, artisans, common laborers, servants, entertainers, criminals, and slaves or bonded workers do not experience the law in the same way.

Family reputation is extremely important. A scandal can damage parents, siblings, marriage prospects, employment, and political allies. Adoption, concubinage, illegitimacy, and uncertain parentage can all become sensitive issues depending on circumstance.

Punishment can be severe. Imprisonment, beating, dismissal, confiscation, forced labor, exile, execution, and punishment of relatives are all plausible consequences for serious crimes. The higher the political stakes, the less likely justice is to resemble an impartial modern legal process.

Confession, witness testimony, written records, seals, physical evidence, and official reputation matter, but investigations can be distorted by status. A servant accusing a noble needs extraordinary proof or protection.

## Women, Marriage, and Family

Marriage is often a family and political arrangement rather than a purely romantic choice. Noble marriages join households and create alliances. Concubinage is socially accepted among powerful men, while women's sexual behavior is judged far more strictly.

A woman's prospects depend heavily on birth, reputation, fertility, household connections, and education. Women can still wield substantial informal power through management, patronage, commerce, family networks, medical knowledge, or proximity to influential people.

The Rear Palace represents an extreme version of these pressures. Beauty and refinement are assets, but pregnancy can change a woman's rank overnight, and rivalry between households can become dangerous.

## The Pleasure District

The capital's pleasure district is a recognized part of urban life rather than a hidden criminal underworld. It contains brothels, teahouses, entertainment houses, restaurants, merchants, musicians, courtesans, servants, guards, apothecaries, and ordinary residents.

High-ranking courtesans are trained entertainers whose value may depend on beauty, conversation, music, poetry, games, etiquette, and reputation as much as sex. Wealthy clients may spend enormous sums for access or exclusivity.

The district also has its own social hierarchy, debts, contracts, rivalries, and protections. Workers may have varying degrees of freedom. Some hope to earn enough to leave; others may be bought out by patrons or trapped by debt.

Because wealthy officials, merchants, soldiers, and nobles visit the district, information travels quickly. Gossip heard in an entertainment house may later affect palace politics.

## Economy and Daily Life

The empire uses coinage and market trade alongside household production, taxes, stipends, gifts, and barter. Large cities support specialized merchants selling medicine, spices, cloth, jewelry, paper, food, books, cosmetics, tools, and imported luxuries.

Most people outside the capital live more modestly. Agriculture supports the state, and harvest failures can increase taxes, debt, migration, banditry, disease, and unrest.

Imported goods such as rare spices, glass, unusual medicines, foreign fabrics, fruits, perfumes, and gemstones signal wealth and international trade. Their unfamiliarity can also create medical or political problems when people do not understand how to use them safely.

## Education, Literacy, and Records

Literacy is valuable and unevenly distributed. Officials, scholars, merchants, physicians, clerks, and upper-class households are far more likely to read and write than ordinary laborers.

Written documents carry authority. Seals, signatures, ledgers, medical texts, official notices, family records, contracts, and correspondence can become critical evidence. Forgery and altered records are therefore serious tools of intrigue.

Knowledge itself is a form of power. A servant who can read a ledger, identify an herb, recognize a noble crest, or understand court protocol may know something their social rank suggests they should not.

## Religion, Ritual, and Belief

The setting includes temples, shrines, ritual specialists, funeral customs, ancestral respect, fortune telling, taboos, and beliefs about spirits or curses. Religion is woven into daily custom rather than organized around one clearly defined universal church.

Ritual can carry political meaning. Auspicious dates, ceremonies, imperial rites, mourning practices, and symbols of legitimacy matter to the court. Even officials who privately doubt supernatural claims may respect ritual because society expects it.

## Foreign Lands and Trade

The empire is powerful but not isolated. Foreign merchants, diplomats, travelers, medicines, foods, fabrics, technologies, and customs appear through trade and diplomatic contact.

Border regions and western territories may differ significantly from the capital in language, clothing, food, ethnicity, and customs. Foreign knowledge can be valuable precisely because court physicians and officials may not recognize it.

Diplomatic marriages, tribute, trade agreements, military threats, and disputes over succession can connect palace intrigue to events far beyond the capital.

## Crime, Espionage, and Intrigue

Most conflicts are not resolved through open combat. Information is the central weapon of the setting. Servants overhear conversations. Physicians know secrets. Courtesans learn what officials say when relaxed. Eunuchs control access. Clerks control records. Merchants know who is buying unusual goods.

Blackmail, hidden parentage, forged correspondence, poison, sabotage, concealed illness, bribery, theft, smuggling, and manipulation of servants are all believable threats.

Investigations should therefore focus on opportunity, social access, motive, physical evidence, medical facts, contradictions in testimony, and who benefits from silence.

## Tone and GM Principles

Run the world as grounded historical mystery and social drama. There are no conventional supernatural powers. Apparent curses should usually have natural, social, medical, or criminal explanations unless canon explicitly establishes otherwise.

Status matters constantly. A correct accusation made by the wrong person can still be dangerous. A servant may need a patron to act on information. A noble may avoid punishment unless the evidence is undeniable or a more powerful figure intervenes.

The palace should feel beautiful, orderly, and dangerous at the same time. Silk, gardens, incense, fine food, music, and ceremony coexist with disease, coercion, jealousy, political calculation, and fragile human health.

Everyday life matters as much as major intrigue. Cooking, medicine, gossip, markets, festivals, servants' routines, laundry, correspondence, court etiquette, and household logistics should regularly create opportunities for play.

Canon establishes the world and its institutions, not a fixed sequence of events. Once player actions diverge from the original story, follow consequences naturally while preserving the setting's social hierarchy, political incentives, medical limitations, and grounded tone.

## Knowledge Boundaries

Established facts include the imperial monarchy, the division between Outer and Rear Palace, the consort hierarchy, palace servants and eunuchs, practical herbal medicine, widespread social inequality, the importance of family reputation, and a culture where poison and illness can shape politics.

Common belief includes curses, omens, unlucky events, supernatural causes of disease, and assumptions based on rank or gender. These beliefs may be sincerely held even when incorrect.

Expert interpretation comes from physicians, apothecaries, scholars, officials, experienced servants, merchants, and specialists. Experts can still be wrong, especially outside their field.

Rumor should be treated as information rather than truth. Palace gossip, pleasure-district stories, accusations of infidelity, claims of poisoning, secret parentage, and political conspiracy may be accurate, distorted, or deliberately planted.

Keep concealed any information that ordinary characters would not reasonably know. The motives of senior officials, private affairs of the imperial family, secret illnesses, hidden relationships, and ongoing conspiracies must be discovered through play rather than supplied as common lore.